  "live_records": {
    "q-9709": {
      "dom_id": "step-q9709",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/551cca083ab3e8ac624d119b5539574a79636b83c63574880249e52f198bfcb5.png)\n\nFind the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )$ and $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/d0ebb3b99f66e8c1af93f1a15ff4284f5fb9a83fb4d91bad1027e18c8b447939.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))^{2}-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}((\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(0-0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi +\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
