    "q-126408": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126408",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/ea8b9532d6b2b9318f77155a585d46dd18cb168c455318bd5abe72c4a11a4500.png)\n\nFind the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ and $r=1,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "Consider the mostright petal along $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/a1129012105492fb870dbbc5960008b984ea98a9f61c267b92c1b0d695ee838c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )))^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})+(\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12})+\\frac{1}{12}(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total shaded area is\n\n$$\nA=3{A}_{1}=3(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{12})=2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
