    "q-353117": {
      "dom_id": "step-q353117",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/eec15c94d63cab750a0dc15525d800452a1a1dbdd3e948dff8ef8d6cf0e2abc7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14248834/assets/9bcc7175d07a6d9bb4c3fa202f3ad711effa962e3c9a798b3869f9601d4214c3.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}((\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
